        'text' => "One day in spring, four lawyers were riding along a muddy country road on their way to court. They talked and laughed as they went, a jolly company.\n\nPassing through a grove of small trees, they heard a great fluttering overhead and a feeble chirping in the wet grass by the roadside.\n\n\"The storm has blown two baby robins out of their nest,\" said Mr. Hardin. \"They are too young to fly.\"\n\n\"What a pity! They will die down there in the grass,\" said Mr. Speed.\n\n\"Oh, well,\" answered Mr. Hardin. \"They are nothing but birds. Why should we bother?\"\n\nThe three men looked down at the little birds fluttering in the cold grass, saw the mother robin flying about and crying to her mate, then rode on laughing as before. In a few minutes they had forgotten the birds entirely.\n\nBut the fourth lawyer stopped. His name was Abraham Lincoln. He climbed down from his horse and lifted the little ones up gently in his big warm hands. Their nest sat high in the tree, far above his reach, but Lincoln could climb. Up he went, the way he had climbed so many trees as a boy, and one by one he tucked the chicks into their cozy home, where two more were waiting. All cuddled down together and were happy.\n\nWhen he joined the others, his shoes were covered with mud and his coat was torn by thorns.\n\n\"Where have you been?\" asked Mr. Hardin.\n\n\"I stopped a minute to give those birds to their mother,\" said Lincoln.\n\n\"Well, we always thought you were a hero,\" laughed Mr. Speed. \"Now we know it.\"\n\n\"Gentlemen,\" said Mr. Lincoln, \"I could not have slept tonight if I had left those helpless little robins to die in the wet grass.\"\n\nThe laughing lawyer became in time President of the United States, and next to Washington he stands as the greatest of Americans.",
