        'text' => "Long ago the proud tyrant Gessler ruled over the people of Switzerland and made their lives bitter. One day he set his own cap atop a tall pole in the public square and ordered every man who entered the town to bow down before it.\n\nOne man would not bow. His name was William Tell, and he stood straight with folded arms, laughing at the swinging cap.\n\nWhen Gessler heard of it he was furious, and afraid too, for if one man disobeyed, others might follow. He knew that Tell was the finest marksman in all the land, so he planned a test of cruel cleverness. Tell's own little boy must stand in the square with an apple on his head, and Tell must shoot the apple off with one arrow.\n\n\"Will you make me kill my boy?\" cried Tell.\n\n\"Say no more,\" said Gessler. \"You must hit the apple. If you fail, my soldiers shall kill the boy before your eyes.\"\n\nThen without another word Tell chose an arrow and bent his great bow. The boy stood firm and still, for his faith in his father's skill was perfect.\n\nThe arrow whistled through the air and struck the apple fairly in the center, carrying it clean away. The people shouted with joy.\n\nAs Tell turned to leave, a second arrow, hidden beneath his coat, dropped to the ground.\n\n\"Fellow!\" cried Gessler. \"What means this second arrow?\"\n\n\"Tyrant!\" answered Tell. \"This arrow was for your heart if I had hurt my child.\"\n\nThe old story goes that not long after, Tell's arrow did bring down Gessler, and Switzerland was free.",
