        'synopsis' => 'A tyrant grants a condemned man one last farewell and holds his dearest friend hostage, wagering a life against a promise.',
        'text' => "Long ago there lived two friends named Damon and Pythias, who loved each other so dearly that ever after, men used their names as a sign of perfect friendship. They had grown up together, had studied together, and shared everything they had, so that people said two bodies went with one soul.\n\nPythias spoke openly against Dionysius, the tyrant of his city, and for this he was dragged to prison. A day was set for him to die. His home lay far away, and he begged one last favor.\n\n\"Give me leave to go and say good-by to my father and mother,\" he said, \"and then I will come back and give up my life.\"\n\nThe tyrant only laughed.\n\n\"How can I know that you will keep your promise?\" he said. \"You want nothing but to cheat me and save yourself.\"\n\nThen Damon stepped forward from among the friends of the prisoner.\n\n\"O king,\" he said, \"shut me in prison in my friend's place, and let Pythias go to bid his people farewell. He has never in his life broken his word. If he is not here on the day you have set, then I will die in his stead.\"\n\nThe tyrant marveled that any man should make such an offer, yet he agreed. Damon was chained in the cell while Pythias sailed for his old home.\n\nThe days slipped past, and the morning appointed for the death arrived with Pythias still absent. The jailer kept close watch on Damon, but Damon made no move toward escape. His faith stood firm.\n\n\"If Pythias does not come,\" he said quietly, \"it will be no fault of his own. Some storm or accident holds him back against his will.\"\n\nAt last came the very hour. Damon was led out ready to die, sorrowing only that his friend might be thought false. The tyrant himself watched from his seat, half in anger and half in wonder, for never had he seen a man so calm at such an hour. And at that very moment Pythias burst through the door. Storms and shipwreck had well-nigh cost him his life, yet here he stood. He greeted Damon gladly, then gave himself into the hands of the jailer, happy that he had come in time to keep his word.\n\nEven the heart of a tyrant could read such a lesson. Dionysius rose from his seat and came down among them, for he saw that two men who trusted each other so wholly deserved not to die.\n\n\"I would give all my wealth,\" he said, \"to have one such friend.\"\n\nAnd he set them both free.\n\nThe story of those two spread through all the world, and it is told to this day, so that men may learn what true friendship is worth.",
