        'synopsis' => 'A runaway slave nurses a wounded lion back to health in a cave, and years later the grateful beast remembers him before all of Rome.',
        'text' => "In Rome long ago there lived a poor slave named Androclus. His master was so cruel that at last Androclus ran away and hid in a wild forest, where food was so scarce that he grew weak and sick and thought he would die. One day he crept into a cave and lay down to rest.\n\nA great roar woke him. A lion had entered the cave! Androclus waited for death, but the beast did not attack. It limped, and held up one swollen paw. Taking heart, Androclus drew the paw gently toward him and found a long sharp thorn buried in it. He gripped the end firmly and pulled it out.\n\nThe lion was full of joy. It jumped about like a dog and licked the hands of its new friend, rubbing its great head against his shoulder. From that day the two lived together in the cave. Each day the lion brought Androclus food, and each night they slept side by side.\n\nAt last soldiers passing through the wood caught the runaway and carried him back to Rome. The law was that every slave who fled his master must fight a hungry lion in the arena, so a fierce lion was shut up without food while the crowds were called together.\n\nWhen the day came, thousands pressed in to see the sport, just as people today crowd to a circus or a ball game. The door opened, and pale Androclus was led forth into the dusty ring. He looked up at the thousands of faces around him and found no pity in any of them. Then the starving lion rushed in and bounded toward him with a single spring.\n\nAndroclus gave a great cry, but it was not a cry of fear. It was a cry of gladness, for he knew his old friend of the cave. The lion stopped short, crouched at his feet like a gentle dog, and licked them lovingly, rubbing its head against him just as it used to do in the cave.\n\nThe astonished people rose as one man, unable to understand what they saw. Then Androclus stood up and put his arm around the lion's neck.\n\n\"I am a man,\" he said, \"yet no man was ever kind to me. Only this poor lion befriended me, and we love each other as brothers.\"\n\nThen the whole city cried out with one voice.\n\n\"Live and be free!\"\n\n\"Let the lion go free too!\"\n\nAnd so Androclus won his liberty, and the lion was given to him for his own. For many years they lived together in Rome, and no one ever troubled them again.",
