    [
        'slug' => 'nature-bingo',
        'gutenberg_id' => 3031,
        'title' => 'Bingo',
        'author' => 'Ernest Thompson Seton',
        'license' => 'Public domain',
        'genres' => ['Science/Nature'],
        'quiz_difficulty' => 'hard',
        'cover' => 'covers/nature-bingo.jpg',
        'synopsis' => 'A clumsy black puppy grows into the strangest dog on the Manitoba prairie, a wolf-hearted friend who saves his master\'s life and dies at his door.',
        'text' => "Early in November 1882, on the Manitoba prairie, I watched our neighbor's collie fight a wild wolf and hold it at bay until I rode up to end the battle. I begged to buy so splendid a dog.\n\n\"Why don't you try to buy one of the children?\" his owner scoffed.\n\nSo I took the next best thing, one of his supposed sons, a roly-poly ball of black fur that looked more like a bear cub than a puppy. We named him Bingo.\n\nThat winter he grew fat, clumsy, and full of mischief, and in spring his education began. He learned to fetch our yellow cow from the unfenced prairie, then loved the work too well. Soon he drove old Dunne home a dozen times a day, whether she wanted coming or not, until the poor cow grew thin and nervous and Bingo had to be forbidden the game altogether.\n\nHe taught me things in return. Halfway to town stood a certain corner post, and Bingo never passed it without careful study. I learned that dogs and wolves across the whole country used such posts as a kind of newspaper, where each visitor left news in scent for all who followed. Once every hair along his back stood up as he read a track there, and he backed away growling low. It was, I found out, the mark of a timber wolf, the only thing on the plains he truly feared.\n\nHis one public appearance as a working collie ended in ruin. I entered him in the trial at the Carberry Fair, and when the judge gave the word, off went Bing after old Dunne at racing speed. But the two had rehearsed all summer without meaning to. The cow bolted straight for her stable two miles away with Bingo whooping behind, and neither dog nor cow was seen again that day. The prize went to the only other entry.\n\nYet for all his wolfish heart, his faith was mine. After two years away I came back a stranger, and that very week Bing crawled home dragging a steel wolf trap with a log chained to it, so savage with pain that no man dared come near. I walked up and took hold of the iron.\n\n\"Bing, don't you know me?\"\n\nHe had seized my wrist in his teeth, but at my voice he let go at once and never resisted again.\n\nMy strangest memory of him came that same spring. Careless after long luck, I sprang a steel trap across my own hand on Kennedy's Plain, and struggling blindly, sprang another upon my foot. There I lay staked to the earth, a mile from any help. Night fell. Prairie wolves gathered out of the gloom and sat in a circle around me, edging closer as my strength drained away. My pony would not leave, and no one knew where I had gone.\n\nSuddenly a great black shape burst from the darkness with a roar that scattered the pack like chaff. It leaped straight at me, and then noble Bingo rubbed his shaggy sides against me and licked my cold face.\n\n\"Fetch me the trap wrench, Bing!\"\n\nHe brought my rifle first, then my sash, and at last the wrench, wagging his tail in triumph. One turn of the nut freed my hand, another freed my foot, and we went home together at a gallop.\n\nBing lived the wild life he loved, and it ended as such lives do. Poison laid for wolves proved stronger than he was, and feeling the pang, he did not drag himself toward the neighbors who owned him now. He set out to find me. I was away that night, and the next day I found him dead in the snow, his head resting on the sill of my door, the door of his puppyhood days.\n\nHe was my dog to the last, in his heart of hearts.",
