    [
        'slug' => 'nature-silverspot',
        'gutenberg_id' => 3031,
        'title' => 'Silverspot the Crow',
        'author' => 'Ernest Thompson Seton',
        'license' => 'Public domain',
        'genres' => ['Science/Nature'],
        'quiz_difficulty' => 'medium',
        'cover' => 'covers/nature-silverspot.jpg',
        'synopsis' => 'For twenty years one wise old crow led two hundred of his kind above Toronto, until the one enemy he always warned them about ended his long reign.',
        'text' => "How many of us have truly known a wild animal? Not met one once or twice, but known it, season upon season, while it stayed free? I did. He was a crow.\n\nSilverspot took his name from a silvery white patch between his right eye and his bill, a mark like a small coin that let me tell him from every other crow in the country and piece together his story. He was chief of some two hundred crows whose fortress was Castle Frank, a pine-clad hill on the edge of Toronto.\n\nTwice each day the old fellow flew down a certain wooded ravine with his band strung out behind him. An old resident of the neighborhood told me once, \"That there crow has been flying up and down this hollow for more than twenty years.\" Crows keep a spoken language, and Silverspot commanded it like a general. One windy morning I heard his contented call that meant all was well and come along. The next day I pointed my walking stick at the passing troop, and at once his cry changed to danger, so that all rose higher. On the third day I carried a gun, and his voice rang out a warning that sent every crow towering fifty feet above me, far beyond gunshot, before they sank again to the shelter of the valley. When a hawk appeared, the cry changed yet again, and instead of scattering the band bunched into a solid black body that no hawk would dare to strike. A very little difference in sound made a very great difference in meaning.\n\nSummer was school time in the pines of Castle Frank. There the young crows were drilled daily by their old teacher, who could be strict as any sergeant. I have watched him put his company through their signals, fly, mount, bunch up, scatter, form line, descend, forage, each command obeyed in a rush of wings while trusted sentries kept watch apart. The youngsters learned what guns and traps meant, learned that an umbrella is not a gun, that a farmer's wife is far less dangerous than her fifteen-year-old son, and how to count, though their master himself could count nearly to thirty.\n\nThe old chief had his human weaknesses too. He kept a secret hoard of bright things buried by the brook, white pebbles and clam shells and the handle of a china cup, which he would spread out in the sun and gloat over like a miser. And once I saw him lose a piece of bread down a bricked-over sewer, fly at once to the far end of the tunnel, and wait for the current to carry his treasure back into daylight. No crow but Silverspot would have thought of that.\n\nBut a crow has one enemy he cannot outwit, the owl, and only one foolish hour, the night. In the winter of 1893 I found blood on the snow near Castle Frank and beside it the great double-toed print of a horned owl. Two days later, at dawn, black feathers drifted over the snow and the whole band filled the woods with cries of sorrow. Beneath the torn remains, half buried in the drift, I uncovered a head with the silvery mark between eye and bill.\n\nIt was old Silverspot himself, slain at last by the very enemy he had taught hundreds of young crows to fear.\n\nThe old nest on Sugar Loaf hill stands empty now. Crows still come each spring to Castle Frank, but without their famous leader the band dwindles year by year, and soon they will be seen about the old pines no more.",
