    [
        'slug' => 'romance-busy-broker',
        'gutenberg_id' => 2776,
        'title' => 'The Romance of a Busy Broker',
        'author' => 'O. Henry',
        'license' => 'Public domain',
        'genres' => ['Romance'],
        'quiz_difficulty' => 'hard',
        'cover' => 'covers/romance-busy-broker.jpg',
        'synopsis' => 'A Wall Street broker works like a machine until a scent of lilac through the window makes him propose in the middle of the market rush.',
        'text' => "Pitcher, the confidential clerk in the office of Harvey Maxwell, broker, allowed a look of mild surprise to visit his usually expressionless face when his employer briskly entered at half past nine in company with his young lady stenographer. With a snappy \"Good-morning, Pitcher,\" Maxwell dashed at his desk and plunged into the great heap of letters and telegrams waiting there.\n\nThe young lady had been Maxwell's stenographer for a year. She was beautiful in a way that was decidedly unstenographic. Her dress was gray and plain, but it fitted her with fidelity and discretion, and in her neat black turban hat was the gold-green wing of a macaw. This morning she was softly and shyly radiant, and instead of going straight to her desk she lingered in the outer office, once drifting close enough for Maxwell to feel her there.\n\nBut the man at that desk was no longer quite a man. It was a busy New York broker, moved by buzzing wheels and uncoiling springs.\n\n\"Well, what is it?\" asked Maxwell sharply.\n\n\"Nothing,\" answered the stenographer, moving away with a little smile.\n\nAt the door she paused by Pitcher. \"Did Mr. Maxwell say anything yesterday about engaging another stenographer?\"\n\n\"He did,\" said Pitcher. \"I notified the agency to send a few samples this morning. You are the first one on the list.\"\n\n\"I will do the work as usual, then, until somebody comes,\" said the young lady, and she hung the black turban hat in its accustomed place.\n\nWhoever has never seen a Manhattan broker during a rush of business is handicapped for the profession of anthropology. The poet sings of the crowded hour of glorious life, but the broker's hour is not merely crowded. The minutes and seconds are hanging to all the straps and packing both platforms.\n\nAnd this day was Harvey Maxwell's busiest. The ticker reeled out its tape, the desk telephone buzzed without pause, messenger boys ran in and out, and men thronged the rail calling orders, jovially, sharply, viciously. On the Exchange there were hurricanes and landslides and volcanoes that morning, all reproduced in miniature in the broker's office. Maxwell jumped from ticker to telephone, from desk to door, with the trained agility of a harlequin. Stocks and bonds, loans and margins, a whole world of finance whirled through that room, and there was no room in it for any other world at all.\n\nInto the middle of this stress came Pitcher, leading a young lady with a high-rolled fringe of golden hair and a string of beads as large as hickory nuts.\n\n\"Lady from the Stenographer's Agency to see about the position,\" announced Pitcher.\n\n\"What position?\" asked Maxwell, frowning, his hands full of papers.\n\n\"Position of stenographer. You told me yesterday to call them and have one sent over.\"\n\n\"You are losing your mind, Pitcher,\" cried Maxwell. \"Why should I have given you such an order? Miss Leslie has given perfect satisfaction for a year. The place is hers as long as she chooses to keep it. Cancel that order with the agency, and bring no more of them in here!\"\n\nThe silver heart at the visitor's neck banged indignantly against the furniture as she departed. Pitcher remarked to the bookkeeper that the old man seemed more absent-minded every day.\n\nThe pace grew fiercer toward noon, and then came a slight lull in the uproar. Maxwell stood by his desk with telegrams in his hands, a fountain pen over his ear, his hair hanging in disorderly strings. The window was open, for the janitress Spring had turned on a little warmth that morning, and through it came wandering a delicate sweet odor of lilac.\n\nThe odor belonged to Miss Leslie. It was her own, and hers only. The world of finance dwindled suddenly to a speck. And she was in the next room, twenty steps away.\n\n\"By George, I'll do it now,\" said Maxwell half aloud. \"I'll ask her now. I wonder I didn't do it long ago.\"\n\nHe dashed into the inner office and charged upon her desk. She looked up with a smile, and a soft pink crept into her cheek.\n\n\"Miss Leslie,\" he began hurriedly, \"I have but a moment to spare. I want to say something in that moment. Will you be my wife? I have not had time to make love to you in the ordinary way, but I truly love you. Talk quick, please. Those fellows are clubbing the stuffing out of Union Pacific.\"\n\n\"Oh, what are you talking about?\" exclaimed the young lady, rising to her feet, round-eyed.\n\n\"Don't you understand?\" pressed Maxwell. \"I want you to marry me. I love you, Miss Leslie. They are calling me to the telephone now. Tell them to wait a minute, Pitcher. Won't you, Miss Leslie?\"\n\nThe stenographer acted very oddly. First she seemed struck with amazement. Then tears flowed from her wondering eyes, and then she smiled sunnily through them and slid one arm tenderly about the broker's neck.\n\n\"I know now,\" she said softly. \"It is this old business that has driven everything else out of your head. I was frightened at first. But don't you remember, Harvey? We were married last evening at eight o'clock in the Little Church Around the Corner.\"",
        'questions' => [
            ['section' => 'Vocabulary', 'type' => 'mcq', 'question_text' => 'What is a "stenographer"?', 'option_a' => 'One who writes down speech in shorthand and types it', 'option_b' => 'One who reads stock prices from a tape', 'option_c' => 'One who delivers telegrams on foot', 'option_d' => 'One who keeps the accounts of a firm', 'correct_option' => 'a'],
            ['section' => 'Vocabulary', 'type' => 'identification', 'question_text' => 'Which word names a sweet-smelling spring flower whose scent came through the window? The odor belonged to Miss Leslie alone.', 'acceptable_answers' => ['lilac', 'lilacs']],
            ['section' => 'Comprehension', 'type' => 'true_false', 'question_text' => 'Pitcher guessed rightly why Miss Leslie looked so radiant that morning.', 'correct_option' => 'b'],
            ['section' => 'Comprehension', 'type' => 'sequence', 'question_text' => 'Put the events of the story in order.', 'sequence_items' => ['Broker Maxwell dashes in at half past nine accompanied by his stenographer Miss Leslie who lingers shyly radiant', 'She asks Pitcher about a new stenographer though she means to keep her place', 'The busiest day of the year roars around Maxwell like a machine of wheels and springs', 'Pitcher leads in an agency girl and is sharply told that Miss Leslie has given perfect satisfaction', 'In a noonday lull the scent of lilac moves him to propose and he learns they were married the evening before']],
            ['section' => 'Comprehension', 'type' => 'matching', 'question_text' => 'Match each figure to their part.', 'match_pairs' => [['left' => 'Harvey Maxwell', 'right' => 'Broker so busy he forgets his own wedding'], ['left' => 'Miss Leslie', 'right' => 'Stenographer already married to the man she works for'], ['left' => 'Pitcher', 'right' => 'Confidential clerk surprised to see the boss arrive with his typist'], ['left' => 'The agency girl', 'right' => 'Sample sent over whose beads banged against the furniture as she left']]],
            ['section' => 'Comprehension', 'type' => 'mcq', 'question_text' => 'Why had Pitcher notified the agency for a new stenographer?', 'option_a' => 'Maxwell had told him the day before to call them', 'option_b' => 'Miss Leslie had handed in her resignation', 'option_c' => 'The office work had doubled overnight', 'option_d' => 'He wished to surprise the busy broker with help', 'correct_option' => 'a'],
            ['section' => 'Comprehension', 'type' => 'mcq', 'question_text' => 'What made Maxwell suddenly forget the world of finance?', 'option_a' => 'A delicate sweet odor of lilac drifting through the open window', 'option_b' => 'A crash in Union Pacific shares on the Exchange', 'option_c' => 'A telegram from one of his largest clients', 'option_d' => 'The ringing of the desk telephone at noon', 'correct_option' => 'a'],
