    [
        'slug' => 'romance-springtime-carte',
        'gutenberg_id' => 2776,
        'title' => 'Springtime à la Carte',
        'author' => 'O. Henry',
        'license' => 'Public domain',
        'genres' => ['Romance'],
        'quiz_difficulty' => 'medium',
        'cover' => 'covers/romance-springtime-carte.jpg',
        'synopsis' => 'A poor typewriter girl who trades menus for dinners cries over a bill of fare in spring, because dandelions remind her of a farmer she lost.',
        'text' => "It was a day in March, and Sarah was crying over her bill of fare.\n\nTo account for this you may guess that the lobsters were all out, or that she had ordered onions, or that she had come from a sad play. All such theories being wrong, let the story proceed.\n\nSarah was a freelance typewriter girl in New York, prying open the cold clammy world with an unhandy weapon, for her shorthand was weak and office work passed her by. Her crowning victory was a deal with Schulenberg's Home Restaurant next door to her hall room. She typed his bills of fare, a fresh card for each day, and in return Schulenberg sent three meals a day up to her room by a waiter, with a pencil draft each afternoon of what Fate held for tomorrow's customers.\n\nMutual satisfaction followed. The patrons now knew what their food was called even when its nature puzzled them, and Sarah had food through a cold dull winter, which was the main thing with her.\n\nBut the almanac lied about spring. The snows of January still lay like iron in the crosstown streets, and when hand organs keep playing summer tunes in December weather, the city is still in the clutches of winter.\n\nOne afternoon Sarah sat shivering in her hall bedroom with nothing to do but Schulenberg's menu cards. She rocked in her squeaky willow chair and looked out at the blank brick wall of a box factory, yet through that wall she saw a grassy lane shaded with cherry trees, bordered with raspberry bushes, and golden with dandelions.\n\nFor on a farm the summer before, Sarah had loved a farmer. She had stayed two weeks at Sunnybrook Farm, where she learned to love old Farmer Franklin's son Walter, a modern young man who kept a telephone in his cow house. In that shaded lane Walter had wooed and won her, and there they had woven a crown of yellow dandelions for her hair.\n\nThey were to be married in the spring, at the very first signs of spring, Walter said. And Sarah came back to the city to pound her typewriter.\n\nA knock at the door brought her back from the lane. It was the waiter with the pencil draft of Schulenberg's fare for tomorrow, written in angular letters. Sarah slipped a card into her typewriter and set to work, nimble as always.\n\nThat day the changes ran toward spring. The soups grew lighter, lamb figured among the meats, and the pie list swelled while the richer puddings vanished. Down through the courses her fingers danced, until just above the desserts came the vegetables, carrots and peas, asparagus on toast, corn and succotash, cabbage, and then.\n\nSarah was crying over her bill of fare.\n\nFor two weeks no letter had come from Walter, and the next item on the card was dandelions, dandelions with some kind of egg, bother the egg! Dandelions, whose golden blooms Walter had woven for her hair when he crowned her his queen of love and future bride. Dandelions, the harbingers of spring, and the reminder of her happiest days.\n\nBy and by she forced back her tears. The cards must be finished. But her fingers went on absently, her heart still walking the meadow lane, before it came swiftly back to Manhattan.\n\nAt six the waiter carried away the finished cards. At supper Sarah set aside the dish of dandelions untouched, with a sigh, for love tokens are hard to eat as greens.\n\nAt half past seven the couple in the next room began to quarrel, the man above hunted for a note on his flute, the gas went lower, and by these signs Sarah knew it was time to read. She had scarcely begun when the front door bell rang below, and then a strong voice sounded in the hallway. Sarah jumped for her own door, and reached the top of the stairs just as her farmer came up three steps at a jump and took her into his arms.\n\n\"Oh, why haven't you written?\" cried Sarah.\n\n\"New York is a pretty large town,\" said Walter Franklin. \"I came a week ago to your old address, and I have been hunting for you ever since.\"\n\n\"I did write!\"\n\n\"Never got it! But how did I find you? I dropped into that Home Restaurant this evening. I will own it plainly, I like a dish of greens at this time of year. I ran my eye down the typewritten bill of fare, and when I got below cabbage I turned my chair over and hollered for the proprietor. He told me where you lived.\"\n\n\"I remember,\" sighed Sarah happily. \"Dandelions came below cabbage.\"\n\n\"And I would know that cranky capital W your machine makes anywhere in the world,\" said Franklin.\n\n\"But there is no W in dandelions,\" said Sarah.\n\nThe young man drew the card from his pocket and pointed to one line. There on the first menu of the afternoon, under a rayed splotch where a tear had fallen, between the red cabbage and the stuffed green peppers, her wandering fingers had typed the item.\n\n\"Dearest Walter, with hard-boiled egg.\"",
        'questions' => [
